posts.x:
Switching from a spreadsheet shouldn't mean retyping it.

BioManager's Import from Excel reads your sheet as it is: "DOB" is date of birth, "Cage #" is the cage, ♂ is M. Each match says why, you check a preview, and it can all be undone.

{site}

In a spreadsheet, a sample is just a row. In BioManager it knows the mouse it came from: one click opens that mouse.

Mention mouse 16 in a qPCR notebook page and its genotype and cage show up right there.

{site}

Paper cage cards, but they open the cage.

BioManager prints correctly sized cards for cages, tanks and vials, each with a QR code. Scan it with any phone camera at the rack and that cage opens, ready to edit. Brother and Zebra label printers too.

{site}

Upload a GenBank, FASTA or SnapGene file and BioManager keeps the sequence and its features, with an interactive map you can edit.

And the tube has an address: plasmid boxes sit on the same rack grid as everything else.

Free, no licence key: {site}

posts.linkedin:
Plasmid records usually live in two places: a sequence tool on one computer, and a spreadsheet saying which box the tube is in.

In BioManager, upload a GenBank, FASTA or SnapGene file and it keeps the sequence and its features, with an interactive circular and linear map you can edit. The plasmid's storage box sits on the same rack grid as everything else, so every tube has an address. Viruses made from a plasmid link back to it, both ways.

No licence key, no seat limit. Free and open source: {site}

posts.x:
What if I'm lazy but still want it all recorded?

Snap the page in your lab notebook, or type a sentence, and send it to your AI assistant. Connected to BioManager, it finds the cages and mice and proposes the records. You press Approve.

{site}

Tamoxifen 75 mg/kg i.p., days 1–5.

BioManager works out each mouse's dose (and volume) from its latest weight, reminds you each day, draws the body-weight chart with stars where the groups differ, and exports for Prism or R.

{site}

Most lab plots are three groups, a mean, SEM and a star.

In BioManager's notebook, paste from Excel into a data sheet and get a bar, dot, box or scatter plot with error bars and a t-test, Mann–Whitney or ANOVA. Download as SVG or PNG.

{site}

posts.linkedin:
A lot of everyday lab analysis is small: three groups, a mean with SEM, and whether they differ.

BioManager's notebook has data sheets for exactly that. Paste from Excel or import a CSV, add formula columns (=B/mean(B)*100), and get a bar, dot, box, scatter or line plot with SEM or SD error bars, a fitted line, and a t-test, Mann–Whitney or ANOVA with Holm-corrected pairs and significance stars. Plots download as SVG or PNG, and the data stays in the page next to your methods.

Free and open source: {site}

posts.x:
Requested → ordered → received.

Drag the card when the box arrives, and BioManager offers to add it to Reagents, Antibodies or Viruses with the vendor, catalogue number and lot already filled in. Order again is one click.

{site}

Not a mouse lab? Choose Add database, describe your organism (a frog lives in a tank, comes in clutches, is tracked as groups or individuals), tick what it needs, and BioManager speaks your language.

No code, no migration.

{site}

posts.linkedin:
Lab software tends to assume you work on mice.

In BioManager, Add database builds a database for any organism in your own words: a Xenopus colony where the animal is a frog, the housing a tank and a litter a clutch. Choose how it is counted (individuals, groups with a headcount, or both), tick what it needs (crosses, cohorts, a nursery stage, genotyping, environment logs, cryo inventory, census), add schedules like "wean at P21" that can vary with temperature, and add your own columns. Experiments work on it as they do for mice.

Cell lines, yeast strains, axolotls: anything kept in containers and bred or passaged fits. Free and open source: {site}

posts.x:
BioManager's Home page is a to-do list your colony writes for you: mice past 30 weeks, weanings due, the genotyping queue, vials to flip, reagents expiring, the next 14 days.

Three layouts: Classic, Tracks, Freezer.

{site}

posts.linkedin:
The best feature of a real database over a spreadsheet: it can tell you what to do.

BioManager's Home page lists what needs doing today: mice older than 30 weeks, upcoming weanings, the genotyping queue, fly vials due for flipping, expiring stock, zebrafish tasks, the next 14 days and recent orders. Three layouts: Classic cards, Tracks (the coming weeks on one ruler, a track per kind of work) and Freezer (your racks from above, with a pull list in the order you'd walk the room). Optional daily emails send each person their own list.

Free and open source: {site}

Fly and worm stocks, organised the way they sit in the incubator.

BioManager labels each vial or plate with its genotype and purpose (stock, experiment, cross, progeny), sets crosses, collects eggs or picks progeny into new vials, and says when the progeny will be adults. Flip and chunk schedules follow temperature (every 14 days at 25 °C, every 28 at 18 °C) and show up on Home when due. Each rack's grid says when it was last flipped and when the next is due, red when overdue, with a Flipped today button.

Free and open source: {site}

BioManager's lab notebook lets you sign a page when it should be the record of your work, page by page and only if you choose.

You confirm who you are, the page's exact text is fingerprinted and kept, any live experiment in it is frozen, and it is locked. A lab mate can witness it. To change it, its owner amends it with a reason, which stays in the record with every signature, and a signed page can't be deleted. Every editing session is kept in the version history, compared line by line and restorable.

Free and open source: {site}

BioManager's calendar has experiments and to-dos, with colony dates (weanings, genotyping, sac reminders), fly and worm flips, organism schedules and reagent expiry filled in for you, and your Google Calendar or any ICS subscription alongside.

Protocol timelines: write the steps once in days from day 0 (tamoxifen days 0–4, implant day 14, perfuse day 42), start them for a cohort, and every step lands on the calendar; move day 0 and they all move. Equipment booking refuses double bookings, saying who has it. Time away shows what falls due while you're gone and who covers it. And a private link lets Apple, Google or Outlook Calendar subscribe.

Free and open source: {site}

posts.x:
Tick rows, set a field for all of them, and if it was a mistake, undo it. Unless someone has edited those records since, so their work is never silently lost.

And every change is in the history: genotype: DBH-Cre → ∅.

{site}

posts.linkedin:
Spreadsheets make bulk edits easy and mistakes permanent.

In BioManager you tick rows, then set a field, add them to an experiment or sac them, and every bulk action can be undone, unless someone has edited those records since, so their work is never silently lost. Every create, edit and delete is recorded with who and exactly what changed (genotype: DBH-Cre → ∅), and an import from Excel is one batch you can take back.

Free and open source: {site}

posts.x:
Lab software doesn't have to look like 2004.

BioManager follows macOS conventions, has a full dark mode, and lets each person pick an app icon (mouse, zebrafish, worm, fly, cryobox…) in one of six macaron colours. Works on Windows and Linux too.

{site}

posts.linkedin:
People use software they like looking at. BioManager's interface follows macOS conventions (a translucent sidebar, Finder-style tabs, system type and colours) with a full dark mode, and it works in any modern browser on Windows and Linux too.

Each person can pick their own app icon in Settings: a double helix, mouse, zebrafish, C. elegans, Drosophila, cryobox, microtube or petri dish, in one of six macaron colours, and the app's accent colour follows it.

Free and open source: {site}

Labels in BioManager: Print on chooses a sheet of cards for any printer, or a label printer's roll: Brother QL (62 × 29, 90 × 29, 100 × 62 mm), Zebra (2 × 1, 3 × 1, 4 × 2, 4 × 2.5 in) or cryo-tube labels. It prints one label a page, typed to fit, and remembers each person's choice.

For Zebras, Download for Zebra (.zpl) gives the labels in the printer's own language, or an admin adds the printer's address once and Send to Zebra prints straight away. Tick samples or reagents and press Labels for tube labels.

Free and open source: {site}

